<section class="cf" id="cf-story-{{ section.id }}" aria-labelledby="cf-story-h-{{ section.id }}">
  <div class="cf__wrap">

    <div class="cf__head" id="our-coffee">
      <span class="cf__eyebrow">From our hills in Idukki</span>
      <h2 class="cf__h2" id="cf-story-h-{{ section.id }}">We grow the Robusta. We pick it by hand.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cf__two">
      <div class="cf__portrait">
        {% render 'coffee-photo', name: 'pl-coffee-our-hills', w: 1200, h: 1500, alt: 'Cupped hands holding freshly picked coffee cherries on our coffee plot in Idukki', caption: 'Hand-picked, December to February' %}
      </div>
      <div class="cf__voice">
        <p>Our coffee doesn't start in a factory. It starts on our own land in Idukki, where the Robusta bushes grow in the shade of taller trees, with pepper vines climbing the same trunks. That's how most Kerala farms do it. Nobody here planted coffee in neat rows for a photo. It just grows alongside everything else.</p>
        <p>Picking season runs from December to February. We go bush to bush and pick by hand, mostly the red, ripe cherries. It's slow work. Some mornings the leaves are still wet with mist and your fingers go cold. Then the drying yard fills up, the whole place smells of coffee fruit, and you remember why you do it.</p>
        <p>We are not the only ones waiting for the ripe cherries. If we are late, the squirrels get there first, and the birds go for the reddest ones. At night the palm civet, the <i>marappatti</i>, comes for them too. We don't poison or trap anything. We try to pick on time, and we accept that a share of the crop is theirs. Honestly, if the squirrels like it, we take it as a good sign.</p>
        <p>The cherries dry in the sun, the beans come out, and we roast in small batches, by eye and by nose: watching the colour, listening for the first crack, waiting for the smell to turn from grassy to toasty. Always medium. That's where Robusta stops being harsh and still keeps its body.</p>
        {%- if section.settings.show_photo_note -%}
          <p>Every photo of beans in hands on these pages is ours: our hands, our harvest.</p>
        {%- endif -%}
        <span class="cf__sign">The Pure Leven family, Idukki</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cf__two">
      {% render 'coffee-photo', name: 'pl-coffee-drying', w: 1600, h: 1067, alt: 'Spreading freshly picked coffee cherries on a mat to dry in the sun in Idukki', caption: 'Spreading the cherries to dry in the sun' %}
      <div class="cf__card">
        <span class="cf__eyebrow">What goes on our coffee</span>
        <h3 class="cf__h3">Nothing, mostly</h3>
        <ul class="cf__dont">
          <li>No chemical pesticides. Robusta is a hardy plant, and on our land we haven't needed to spray.</li>
          <li>No chemical fertilisers.</li>
          <li>No polish, colour or flavouring on the beans.</li>
          <li class="is-yes">Hand-picked, sun-dried, roasted in small batches.</li>
          <li class="is-yes">Arabica: we don't grow it. It comes from growers in Chikmagalur, and we roast and pack it ourselves.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="cf__muted">We are not certified organic, so you won't see that word on our packs. Certification is a paid audit we haven't done. What we can tell you is how the coffee is grown: naturally, without chemicals.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cf__head">
      <span class="cf__eyebrow">Coffee in numbers</span>
      <h2 class="cf__h2">Eight facts most coffee drinkers don't know</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cf__stats">
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>3–4 days</b><span>How long a coffee flower lasts. The white blossom smells sweet, a little like jasmine, then it's gone.</span></div>
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>~9 months</b><span>From flower to ripe cherry for Robusta. Arabica takes about 7.</span></div>
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>2 beans</b><span>In a normal coffee cherry. In 5 to 10% of cherries only one grows: the round "peaberry".</span></div>
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>~2×</b><span>The caffeine in Robusta compared with Arabica: about 2.2–2.7% of the bean against 1.2–1.5%.</span></div>
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>95%+</b><span>Share of Wayanad's coffee that is Robusta.</span></div>
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>98%</b><span>Of India's coffee growers are small growers, mostly on plots of 10 acres or less.</span></div>
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>100%</b><span>Of Indian coffee is grown under shade, usually under two layers of trees.</span></div>
      <div class="cf__stat"><b>~60,000</b><span>Small coffee growers in Wayanad alone.</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cf__two">
      <div class="cf__head" id="history">
        <span class="cf__eyebrow">How coffee reached Kerala</span>
        <h2 class="cf__h2">From Ethiopian goats to Idukki's hills</h2>
        <p>The short version: coffee came from Ethiopia, was farmed in Yemen, was smuggled into India by a saint, planted by the British in Wayanad, nearly wiped out by a fungus, and saved by a tougher bean from Africa. The long version is better.</p>
        <ol class="cf__tl">
          <li class="is-legend"><time>c. 850 · legend</time><b>Ethiopia: Kaldi's dancing goats</b><p>A goatherd notices his goats leaping about after eating red berries from a shrub. The story was first printed in 1671, about 800 years later, so take it as legend.</p></li>
          <li><time>1400s</time><b>Yemen: coffee becomes a drink</b><p>Sufi monks brew it to stay awake through night prayers. The port of Mocha becomes the world's coffee market, and fertile beans are not allowed to leave.</p></li>
          <li><time>1600s</time><b>Chikmagalur: seven smuggled beans</b><p>Returning from Mecca, the saint Baba Budan hides seven coffee seeds in his beard (some versions say strapped to his chest) and plants them on the Chandragiri hills. Indian coffee begins there.</p></li>
          <li><time>c. 1800</time><b>Anjarakandy, near Thalassery</b><p>The East India Company starts an experimental coffee plantation in north Kerala.</p></li>
          <li><time>1825</time><b>Up the ghats to Wayanad</b><p>Captain Bevan carries plants from Anjarakandy to Manantoddy, today's Mananthavady. They thrive.</p></li>
          <li><time>1841–1869</time><b>Wayanad's coffee boom</b><p>Parry &amp; Co. starts large-scale planting. By 1869 Wayanad holds about half of South India's 1,20,000 acres of Arabica.</p></li>
          <li><time>1869 onwards</time><b>The rust</b><p>Coffee leaf rust, a fungus that had already ruined Ceylon's coffee, spreads through the estates. Arabica collapses. Many estates switch to tea.</p></li>
          <li><time>Late 1800s</time><b>Robusta arrives</b><p>A rust-tolerant coffee from the forests of Central Africa takes over Wayanad. Today it is over 95% of the district's coffee.</p></li>
          <li><time>1879</time><b>Idukki's High Range</b><p>Still thick forest until planters arrive to grow coffee, cinchona and cardamom. Tea later takes over Munnar.</p></li>
          <li class="is-us"><time>1940s–1950s</time><b>Families move into Idukki</b><p>Tens of thousands of farming families from central Travancore settle the hills, about 70,000 by 1951. They plant tapioca, pepper, cardamom and coffee on small plots. That is where most of Idukki's small coffee gardens come from.</p></li>
          <li><time>1942</time><b>Coffee Board of India</b><p>Set up under the Coffee Act to run the industry.</p></li>
          <li><time>1958</time><b>Indian Coffee House, Thrissur</b><p>Workers take over the Coffee Board's cafés as a co-operative, and Kerala's first Indian Coffee House opens in Thrissur on 8 March.</p></li>
          <li><time>2019</time><b>Wayanad Robusta gets a GI tag</b><p>Along with Coorg, Chikmagalur, Araku Valley and Bababudangiris Arabica.</p></li>
          <li><time>2025</time><b>National recognition</b><p>Wayanad Robusta gets a special mention at the national One District One Product awards, the first for a Kerala product.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="cf__head" id="charts">
        <span class="cf__eyebrow">Charts</span>
        <h2 class="cf__h2">Indian coffee, by the numbers</h2>

        <div class="cf__card cf__chart">
          <h3 class="cf__h3">Where India's coffee grows</h3>
          <p class="cf__muted">2024–25 crop, tonnes. Kerala grows about a fifth, almost all Robusta.</p>
          <div class="cf__bars" role="img" aria-label="Karnataka 2,56,000 tonnes, 70 percent; Kerala 72,950 tonnes, 20 percent; Tamil Nadu 19,275 tonnes, 5 percent; other states about 15,000 tonnes, 4 percent">
            <div class="cf__brow"><span>Karnataka</span><div class="cf__btrack"><span class="cf__bfill is-muted" style="width:100%"></span><span class="cf__bval">2,56,000 t · 70%</span></div></div>
            <div class="cf__brow"><span><b>Kerala</b></span><div class="cf__btrack"><span class="cf__bfill" style="width:28.5%"></span><span class="cf__bval is-out" style="left:calc(28.5% + 6px)">72,950 t · 20%</span></div></div>
            <div class="cf__brow"><span>Tamil Nadu</span><div class="cf__btrack"><span class="cf__bfill is-muted" style="width:7.5%"></span><span class="cf__bval is-out" style="left:calc(7.5% + 6px)">19,275 t · 5%</span></div></div>
            <div class="cf__brow"><span>Others</span><div class="cf__btrack"><span class="cf__bfill is-muted" style="width:6%"></span><span class="cf__bval is-out" style="left:calc(6% + 6px)">~15,000 t · 4%</span></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cf__card cf__chart">
          <h3 class="cf__h3">Robusta vs Arabica in India</h3>
          <p class="cf__muted">2025–26 forecast, about 4,03,000 tonnes in all.</p>
          <div class="cf__stack" role="img" aria-label="Robusta 2,84,875 tonnes, 71 percent; Arabica 1,18,125 tonnes, 29 percent">
            <span class="is-rob" style="width:71%">Robusta 71%</span><span class="is-ara" style="width:29%">Arabica 29%</span>
          </div>
          <p class="cf__muted">Robusta 2,84,875 t · Arabica 1,18,125 t</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cf__card cf__chart">
          <h3 class="cf__h3">What's inside the bean</h3>
          <p class="cf__muted">Typical share of the green bean, %. This is why Robusta tastes bolder and Arabica sweeter.</p>
          <div class="cf__legend"><span><i style="background:var(--cf-arabica)"></i>Arabica</span><span><i style="background:var(--cf-robusta)"></i>Robusta</span></div>
          <div class="cf__bars" role="img" aria-label="Caffeine: Arabica 1.2 to 1.5 percent, Robusta 2.2 to 2.7. Sucrose: Arabica 6 to 9, Robusta 3 to 7. Oil: Arabica 15 to 17, Robusta 10 to 11.5. Chlorogenic acids: Arabica 5.5 to 8, Robusta 7 to 10.">
            {%- assign chem = 'Caffeine|7.5|1.2–1.5%|13.6|2.2–2.7%;Sugar (sucrose)|41.7|6–9%|27.8|3–7%;Oil|88.9|15–17%|59.7|10–11.5%;Chlorogenic acids|37.5|5.5–8%|47.2|7–10%' | split: ';' -%}
