                  {%- assign w = c[1] | plus: 0 -%}<div class="cf__btrack"><span class="cf__bfill is-ara" style="width:{{ c[1] }}%"></span>{%- if w > 70 -%}<span class="cf__bval" style="color:#1A1A1A">{{ c[2] }}</span>{%- else -%}<span class="cf__bval is-out" style="left:calc({{ c[1] }}% + 6px)">{{ c[2] }}</span>{%- endif -%}</div>
                  <div class="cf__btrack"><span class="cf__bfill is-rob" style="width:{{ c[3] }}%"></span><span class="cf__bval is-out" style="left:calc({{ c[3] }}% + 6px)">{{ c[4] }}</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cf__head" id="calendar">
      <span class="cf__eyebrow">A year on a Kerala coffee farm</span>
      <h2 class="cf__h2">From blossom showers to harvest</h2>
      <p>Everything hangs on the March–April "blossom showers". A good rain then, and the bushes turn white with flowers within days. No rain, no crop. Then the cherries take most of the year to ripen, right through the monsoon.</p>
    </div>
    {% render 'coffee-photo', name: 'pl-coffee-blossom', w: 1600, h: 1067, alt: 'White coffee blossoms among dark green coffee leaves', caption: 'Coffee blossom: it lasts three or four days', sizes: '100vw' %}
    <div class="cf__table-wrap" style="padding:14px">
      {%- assign months = 'J,F,M,A,M,J,J,A,S,O,N,D' | split: ',' -%}
      {%- assign cal = 'Harvest, by hand|h|110000000001;Sun-drying|d|111000000000;Blossom showers and flowers|b|001100000000;Cherries growing|g|000011111110;Southwest monsoon|w|000001111000' | split: ';' -%}
      <div class="cf__cal" role="img" aria-label="Harvest December to February; sun-drying January to March; blossom showers and flowering March and April; cherries grow May to November; southwest monsoon June to September">
        <span></span>{%- for m in months -%}<span class="m">{{ m }}</span>{%- endfor -%}
        {%- for row in cal -%}
          {%- assign c = row | split: '|' -%}
          {%- assign flags = c[2] | split: '' -%}
          <span class="r">{{ c[0] }}</span>
          {%- for f in flags -%}<span class="c{% if f == '1' %} on {{ c[1] }}{% endif %}"></span>{%- endfor -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cf__two" id="places">
      <div class="cf__card">
        <span class="cf__eyebrow">Wayanad</span>
        <h3 class="cf__h3">Kerala's coffee country</h3>
        <p>If Kerala has a coffee capital, it's Wayanad. The district has over 67,000 hectares of coffee, about 90% of the state's coffee area, farmed mostly by around 60,000 small growers. More than 95% of it is Robusta, grown at 700 to 2,100 m under shade, with pepper, arecanut and banana in between.</p>
        <dl class="cf__spec">
          <dt>GI tag</dt><dd>Wayanad Robusta, 2019</dd>
          <dt>Taste</dt><dd>Full-bodied, aromatic, a hint of chocolate</dd>
          <dt>Harvest</dt><dd>December to February</dd>
          <dt>Yield</dt><dd>About 1,400 kg/ha rain-fed, 2,500 kg/ha irrigated</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="cf__card">
        {% render 'coffee-photo', name: 'pl-coffee-idukki-heap', w: 1600, h: 1067, alt: 'Ripe Robusta coffee cherries from Idukki heaped on coffee leaves' %}
        <span class="cf__eyebrow">Idukki</span>
        <h3 class="cf__h3">Spice hills, with coffee in between</h3>
        <p>Idukki is better known for cardamom, pepper and Munnar's tea. Coffee here is mostly Robusta on small family plots, growing under the same trees that carry the pepper vines. Most of these gardens were planted by farming families who moved into the hills in the 1940s and 50s.</p>
        <dl class="cf__spec">
          <dt>Coffee</dt><dd>Mostly Robusta, on small mixed farms</dd>
          <dt>Grown with</dt><dd>Pepper, cardamom, arecanut, banana</dd>
          <dt>Harvest</dt><dd>December to February</dd>
          <dt>Ours</dt><dd>Hand-picked, sun-dried, no chemicals</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cf__head" id="why-kerala">
      <span class="cf__eyebrow">Why Kerala coffee</span>
      <h2 class="cf__h2">Six things that make it different</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cf__grid">
      <div class="cf__card"><h3 class="cf__h3">Grown in shade</h3><p>All Indian coffee grows under shade trees, usually two layers of them. The cherries ripen slowly and evenly, and the farm stays a forest of sorts.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><h3 class="cf__h3">Pepper on the same trees</h3><p>Kerala farmers train black pepper vines up the shade trees. Coffee below, pepper above, cardamom in the damp corners: one garden, several crops.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><h3 class="cf__h3">Small hands, small plots</h3><p>98% of India's coffee growers are small growers. In Kerala that means family plots, not big estates, and a lot of the work still done by hand.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><h3 class="cf__h3">Robusta country</h3><p>Kerala's warm, wet, mid-height hills suit Robusta. That's why the state grows about a fifth of India's coffee, almost all of it Robusta, the bean behind strong South Indian filter coffee.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><h3 class="cf__h3">Monsoon in the blood</h3><p>Monsooned Malabar, one of India's most famous coffees, comes from the Malabar coast: beans aged in open warehouses through the monsoon winds until they swell and turn pale gold.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><h3 class="cf__h3">Wildlife on the farm</h3><p>Shade-grown coffee farms in the Western Ghats hold a surprising amount of wildlife: civets, squirrels and fruit-eating birds. They take their share of the cherries. That's part of the deal.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cf__head" id="types">
      <span class="cf__eyebrow">Types of coffee</span>
      <h2 class="cf__h2">Species, India's famous coffees, and what "cherry" and "parchment" mean</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cf__table-wrap">
      <table class="cf__table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Species</th><th scope="col">Comes from</th><th scope="col">In India</th><th scope="col">In the cup</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Arabica</b><br><i>Coffea arabica</i></td><td>Highlands of Ethiopia</td><td>Chikmagalur, Coorg, Araku Valley, Bababudangiri (all GI-tagged)</td><td>Smooth, sweet, aromatic; 1.2–1.5% caffeine</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Robusta</b><br><i>Coffea canephora</i></td><td>Forests of Central and West Africa</td><td>Wayanad (GI-tagged), Idukki, Kodagu, Hassan</td><td>Bold, full body, low acidity; 2.2–2.7% caffeine</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Liberica</b>, incl. Excelsa</td><td>West and Central Africa</td><td>A few pockets in Karnataka; rare</td><td>Large beans, unusual fruity-smoky taste</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="cf__grid">
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__eyebrow">India's special coffees</span><ul class="cf__list"><li><b>Monsooned Malabar:</b> natural beans exposed to the southwest monsoon (June to September) until they swell and lose acidity. GI-tagged.</li><li><b>Mysore Nuggets Extra Bold:</b> India's top grade of large washed Arabica.</li><li><b>Robusta Kaapi Royale:</b> India's top grade of washed Robusta.</li></ul></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__eyebrow">Trade words decoded</span><ul class="cf__list"><li><b>Cherry:</b> the whole fruit is dried in the sun, then hulled. Heavier, fruitier coffee. Most small farms, ours included, do this.</li><li><b>Parchment / Plantation:</b> the fruit is pulped and the beans washed before drying. Cleaner, brighter cup.</li><li><b>Peaberry (PB):</b> the single round bean from a one-seed cherry.</li><li><b>AA, A, AB:</b> size grades, biggest first.</li></ul></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cf__head" id="stories">
      <span class="cf__eyebrow">Stories and legends</span>
      <h2 class="cf__h2">Seven coffee stories worth telling at the table</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cf__stories">
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__tag is-legend">Legend</span><h3 class="cf__h3">Kaldi's dancing goats</h3><p>An Ethiopian goatherd sees his goats dancing after eating red berries, tries them himself, and takes them to a monastery. The monks throw them in the fire as the devil's work; the roasting smell is so good they rake them out and brew them. Lovely story. Almost certainly not true.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__tag is-legend">Legend and history</span><h3 class="cf__h3">Seven beans in a beard</h3><p>Yemen guarded its coffee by exporting only roasted or boiled beans that couldn't sprout. Baba Budan, returning from Mecca, is said to have hidden seven fertile seeds in his beard. Seven, because it was a holy number. Indian Arabica, the story goes, began with those seeds.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__tag">History</span><h3 class="cf__h3">The coffee the monsoon made</h3><p>In the days of sail, Indian coffee spent months in damp wooden holds on the way to Europe. It arrived swollen, pale and mellow, and Europeans grew to love it. When fast steamships ended that, the Malabar coast recreated the voyage on land: open warehouses, monsoon winds, months of waiting.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__tag">History</span><h3 class="cf__h3">How a fungus made Britain drink tea</h3><p>In the 1860s Ceylon was one of the world's great coffee islands. Then coffee leaf rust arrived and the estates died one after another. Planters pulled out the coffee and planted tea, and Ceylon tea was born. The same rust reached Wayanad in 1869, and that is why Kerala grows Robusta today.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__tag">History</span><h3 class="cf__h3">The workers who saved the coffee house</h3><p>In the 1950s the Coffee Board decided to close its India Coffee Houses. The workers, backed by A. K. Gopalan, took them over as co-operatives. Kerala's first Indian Coffee House opened in Thrissur on 8 March 1958. The waiters' fan-shaped turbans are still there.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__tag">Nature</span><h3 class="cf__h3">The civet's cup</h3><p>Asian palm civets eat ripe coffee cherries at night. Researchers at the Central University of Kerala found that Robusta beans passed by civets have a different fat and ester profile from ordinary beans, which may explain the smoother taste. We let the civets eat. We don't sell what comes out.</p></div>
      <div class="cf__card"><span class="cf__tag is-legend">Lore</span><h3 class="cf__h3">Why "degree" coffee?</h3><p>Kumbakonam's famous "degree coffee" has no agreed origin. Some say it meant first-grade, "degree" milk, tested with a lactometer. Some say it's about the thickness of the milk. Some say it was just the town's mark of quality. Everyone agrees it's strong.</p></div>
    </div>

